                     <body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/"><p style="letter-spacing:0in">Additional parental information is needed before we can continue with the processing of the<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes">                        </span>Free Application for Federal</p><p style="letter-spacing:0in">Student Aid(FAFSA). See definition of a parent below.<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span><span style="font-style:italic">Incomplete documents will not be returned. They will be disposed of in a secure manner, per university policy. This will delay processing.</span></p><p style="font-style:italic;letter-spacing:0in"><br/></p><p style="font-weight:bold;text-decoration:underline;letter-spacing:0in">Definition of parent:<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span><span style="text-decoration:none"><span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span></span><span style="font-size:9pt;font-weight:normal;text-decoration:none">For the FAFSA,<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span></span><span style="font-size:9pt;font-weight:normal;font-style:italic;text-decoration:none">"Parent</span><span style="font-size:9pt;font-weight:normal;text-decoration:none">" refers to your biological and/ or adoptive parents (including your step-parent if that person is currently married to your "parent"). The following are</span><span style="font-size:9pt;text-decoration:none"> NOT considered to be a "legal parent" for the FAFSA:</span></p><p style="font-size:9pt;letter-spacing:0in">- Grandparents, foster parents, legal guardians, aunts and uncles are NOT considered parents unless they have legally adopted you.</p><p style="font-size:9pt;letter-spacing:0in">- Any person who<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes">  </span>is not married to your parent and who is not a legal or biological parent.<span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span></p><p style="font-size:9pt;letter-spacing:0in"><span style="xfa-spacerun:yes">  </span></p><p style="font-style:italic;letter-spacing:0in"><span style="xfa-spacerun:yes"> </span>Provide information for both parents if parents' FAFSA marital status is "married" or "unmarried and both parents living together".</p></body>
                  </exData>
